              "id": 5,
              "title": "1990s paradigmatic concerns",
              "content_notes": "At the centre of 1990s paradigmatic concerns is a simple idea: The uploaded NET material places the 1990s within a broader debate about paradigms and the assumptions underlying psychological knowledge. It highlights ontology, epistemology and methodology as essential aspects of a knowledge paradigm, and contrasts positivist assumptions with post-positivist, critical, social-constructionist and phenomenological approaches. In the Indian context, these debates connect with the question of whether psychology should remain tied to imported experimental-analytical assumptions or develop culturally situated approaches.\n\nThe important features are:\n• Paradigms involve assumptions about reality, knowledge and methods of inquiry.\n• The source contrasts positivism with several alternative approaches.\n• The Indian debate is connected to cultural relevance and disciplinary identity.\n• The issue is methodological as well as philosophical.\n• SOURCE BASIS\n• PowerWithin Psychology — knowledge paradigms and the Unit 1 discussion of Indian psychology's identity crisis.\n• COMMON EXAM TRAP\n• A paradigm is broader than a research method: it includes assumptions about what can be known and how knowledge is produced.\n• MEMORY CUE\n• 1990s paradigmatic concerns → Paradigms involve assumptions about reality, knowledge and methods of inquiry • The source contrasts positivism with several alternative approaches • The Indian debate is connected to cultural relevance and disciplinary identity\n\nFor a NET-style question, identify the defining feature first and then distinguish it from the closest alternatives.",
