              "id": 7,
              "title": "2000s Indian psychology in academia",
              "content_notes": "At the centre of 2000s Indian psychology in academia is a simple idea: The source marks the 2000s as a period in which Indian psychology became more explicitly articulated within academic discussion. A major source example is the 2002 National Conference on Yoga and Indian Approaches to Psychology in Pondicherry, where participants issued the Manifesto on Indian Psychology. The manifesto emphasized the content, methods and applied possibilities of Indian psychology. The source also describes Indian approaches such as Yoga, Bhagavad Gita, Buddhism, Sufism and Integral Yoga as significant paradigms for psychological knowledge.\n\nThe important features are:\n• The 2000s are presented as a period of greater academic articulation of Indian psychology.\n• The 2002 Pondicherry conference is a key source example.\n• The Manifesto on Indian Psychology is an important landmark in the source account.\n• Indian psychology is presented through multiple traditions rather than a single doctrine.\n• SOURCE BASIS\n• PowerWithin Psychology — Unit 1, Significant Indian Paradigms on Psychological Knowledge and discussion of the 2002 Manifesto on Indian Psychology.\n• COMMON EXAM TRAP\n• The source describes emergence and academic articulation; it does not imply that Indian psychology replaced mainstream experimental psychology.\n• MEMORY CUE\n• 2000s Indian psychology in academia → The 2000s are presented as a period of greater academic articulation of Indian psychology • The 2002 Pondicherry conference is a key source example • The Manifesto on Indian Psychology is an important landmark in the source account\n\nFor a NET-style question, identify the defining feature first and then distinguish it from the closest alternatives.",
