          "flashcards": "Q: What is Experimental-analytical paradigm?\nA: Experimental-analytical paradigm is a focused concept within “Crisis in psychology due to strict adherence to experimental-analytical paradigm (logical empiricism)”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Experimental-analytical paradigm\nA: definition and scope of Experimental-analytical paradigm; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Logical empiricism?\nA: Logical empiricism is a focused concept within “Crisis in psychology due to strict adherence to experimental-analytical paradigm (logical empiricism)”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Logical empiricism\nA: definition and scope of Logical empiricism; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Crisis in psychology?\nA: Crisis in psychology is a focused concept within “Crisis in psychology due to strict adherence to experimental-analytical paradigm (logical empiricism)”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Crisis in psychology\nA: definition and scope of Crisis in psychology; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application",
          "references": "",
          "detailed_notes": "Study the criticism that an exclusively experimental-analytical orientation can narrow the range of psychological questions and methods. Connect this with the later emergence of alternative paradigms.\n\nATTACHED BOOK CONNECTION\nFoundations of Psychology: The book introduces psychology as the scientific study of behavior and mental life, then traces major historical schools and viewpoints. It presents the goals of scientific psychology—description, explanation, prediction and control—and discusses structuralism, functionalism, Gestalt psychology, psychoanalysis and behaviorism alongside biological, learning, cognitive, sociocultural and humanistic viewpoints. It also distinguishes major fields such as clinical, counseling, developmental, experimental and physiological psychology.",
