              "deep_learning": "At the centre of Critical perspective is a simple idea: The critical perspective in the uploaded material treats knowledge as value-mediated and reality as shaped by social, political, cultural, economic, gender and ethnic forces. It questions taken-for-granted assumptions and conventional social structures. The aim is not only description but a reflexive examination of values and the possibility of action in relation to unjust social systems.\n\nThese features should be learned as parts of one psychological idea rather than as disconnected facts. Knowledge is treated as value-mediated rather than completely value-free. Social and historical forces are part of the account of reality. Critical inquiry examines assumptions and conventional structures. The perspective connects understanding with effective action. SOURCE BASIS. PowerWithin Psychology — Unit 1, Critical Perspective. COMMON EXAM TRAP. The critical perspective differs from positivism in its treatment of values, social context and the status of the researcher and participant. MEMORY CUE. Critical perspective → Knowledge is treated as value-mediated rather than completely value-free • Social and historical forces are part of the account of reality • Critical inquiry examines assumptions and conventional structures.\n\nSource grounding: PowerWithin Psychology — Unit 1, Critical Perspective.",
