          "mcqs": "Q1. Which option is most directly associated with “Research problems”?\nA. Variables\nB. Operational definitions\nC. Hypothesis\nD. Research problems\nAnswer: Research problems\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ2. Which option is most directly associated with “Variables”?\nA. Research problems\nB. Operational definitions\nC. Hypothesis\nD. Variables\nAnswer: Variables\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ3. Which option is most directly associated with “Operational definitions”?\nA. Variables\nB. Hypothesis\nC. Operational definitions\nD. Research problems\nAnswer: Operational definitions\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.",
          "flashcards": "Q: What is Research problems?\nA: Research problems is a focused concept within “Research problems, Variables and Operational Definitions, Hypothesis, Sampling”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Research problems\nA: definition and scope of Research problems; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Variables?\nA: characteristics that can take different values and structure research designs\nQ: Key points for Variables\nA: independent; dependent; extraneous; control\nQ: What is Operational definitions?\nA: specifying exactly how an abstract construct will be observed or measured\nQ: Key points for Operational definitions\nA: measurable indicator; replicable procedure; construct → measure\nQ: What is Hypothesis?\nA: a tentative, specific and testable statement about a relationship or difference\nQ: Key points for Hypothesis\nA: null/alternative; directional/non-directional; testability",
          "references": "",
          "detailed_notes": "Build the research sequence: problem → variables → operational definitions → hypothesis → sampling. Be able to distinguish conceptual variables from their operational definitions.\n\nATTACHED BOOK CONNECTION\nResearch Methods in Psychology: The book presents the scientific method as a systematic way of asking questions, forming hypotheses, collecting evidence and evaluating conclusions. It covers naturalistic observation, clinical and case-study methods, surveys, psychological testing, correlational research and experiments, including the roles of samples, populations, independent and dependent variables, control groups, random processes, reliability and validity. Correlation is treated as a relationship between variables rather than proof of causation.",
