          "mcqs": "Q1. Which option is most directly associated with “Ethics in conducting research”?\nA. None of the above\nB. None of the above\nC. Ethics in conducting research\nD. Ethics in reporting research\nAnswer: Ethics in conducting research\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ2. Which option is most directly associated with “Ethics in reporting research”?\nA. None of the above\nB. None of the above\nC. Ethics in reporting research\nD. Ethics in conducting research\nAnswer: Ethics in reporting research\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.",
          "flashcards": "Q: What is Ethics in conducting research?\nA: Ethics in conducting research is a focused concept within “Ethics in conducting and reporting research”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Ethics in conducting research\nA: definition and scope of Ethics in conducting research; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Ethics in reporting research?\nA: Ethics in reporting research is a focused concept within “Ethics in conducting and reporting research”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Ethics in reporting research\nA: definition and scope of Ethics in reporting research; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application",
          "references": "",
          "detailed_notes": "Study ethical conduct during participation, data collection, analysis, authorship and reporting. Focus on integrity, participant protection and responsible reporting.\n\nATTACHED BOOK CONNECTION\nResearch Methods in Psychology: The book presents the scientific method as a systematic way of asking questions, forming hypotheses, collecting evidence and evaluating conclusions. It covers naturalistic observation, clinical and case-study methods, surveys, psychological testing, correlational research and experiments, including the roles of samples, populations, independent and dependent variables, control groups, random processes, reliability and validity. Correlation is treated as a relationship between variables rather than proof of causation.",
          "source_scope": "Authoritative UGC NET Psychology syllabus + Prompts for notes.docx + Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide. Generated practice is PYQ-style; attached-book self-test items are source-based.",
          "bookmarks": false,
          "review_date": "",
