          "pyqs": "PYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 1\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Observation”?\nA. Survey\nB. Interview\nC. Questionnaires\nD. Observation\nAnswer: Observation\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.\n\nPYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 2\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Survey”?\nA. Questionnaires\nB. Survey\nC. Observation\nD. Interview\nAnswer: Survey\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.",
          "mcqs": "Q1. Which option is most directly associated with “Observation”?\nA. Observation\nB. Survey\nC. Interview\nD. Questionnaires\nAnswer: Observation\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ2. Which option is most directly associated with “Survey”?\nA. Interview\nB. Questionnaires\nC. Survey\nD. Observation\nAnswer: Survey\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ3. Which option is most directly associated with “Interview”?\nA. Observation\nB. Survey\nC. Questionnaires\nD. Interview\nAnswer: Interview\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.",
          "flashcards": "Q: What is Observation?\nA: Observation is a focused concept within “Methods of research: Observation, Survey (Interview, Questionnaires), Experimental, Quasi-experimental, Field studies, Cross-Cultural Studies, Phenomenology, Grounded theory, Focus groups, Narratives, Case studies, Ethnography”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Observation\nA: definition and scope of Observation; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Survey?\nA: Survey is a focused concept within “Methods of research: Observation, Survey (Interview, Questionnaires), Experimental, Quasi-experimental, Field studies, Cross-Cultural Studies, Phenomenology, Grounded theory, Focus groups, Narratives, Case studies, Ethnography”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Survey\nA: definition and scope of Survey; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Interview?\nA: Interview is a focused concept within “Methods of research: Observation, Survey (Interview, Questionnaires), Experimental, Quasi-experimental, Field studies, Cross-Cultural Studies, Phenomenology, Grounded theory, Focus groups, Narratives, Case studies, Ethnography”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Interview\nA: definition and scope of Interview; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Questionnaires?\nA: Questionnaires is a focused concept within “Methods of research: Observation, Survey (Interview, Questionnaires), Experimental, Quasi-experimental, Field studies, Cross-Cultural Studies, Phenomenology, Grounded theory, Focus groups, Narratives, Case studies, Ethnography”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Questionnaires\nA: definition and scope of Questionnaires; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application",
          "references": "",
          "detailed_notes": "Create a method matrix. For every named method, learn purpose, data source, typical procedure, strengths and limitations.\n\nATTACHED BOOK CONNECTION\nResearch Methods in Psychology: The book presents the scientific method as a systematic way of asking questions, forming hypotheses, collecting evidence and evaluating conclusions. It covers naturalistic observation, clinical and case-study methods, surveys, psychological testing, correlational research and experiments, including the roles of samples, populations, independent and dependent variables, control groups, random processes, reliability and validity. Correlation is treated as a relationship between variables rather than proof of causation.",
          "source_scope": "Authoritative UGC NET Psychology syllabus + Prompts for notes.docx + Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide. Generated practice is PYQ-style; attached-book self-test items are source-based.",
