          "mcqs": "Q1. Which option is most directly associated with “Parametric tests”?\nA. Sign test\nB. Parametric tests\nC. t-test\nD. Non-parametric tests\nAnswer: Parametric tests\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ2. Which option is most directly associated with “t-test”?\nA. Parametric tests\nB. Non-parametric tests\nC. Sign test\nD. t-test\nAnswer: t-test\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ3. Which option is most directly associated with “Non-parametric tests”?\nA. Sign test\nB. Non-parametric tests\nC. Parametric tests\nD. t-test\nAnswer: Non-parametric tests\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.",
          "flashcards": "Q: What is Parametric tests?\nA: Parametric tests is a focused concept within “Parametric (t-test) and Non-parametric tests (Sign Test, Wilcoxon Signed rank test, Mann-Whitney test, Kruskal-Wallis test, Friedman)”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Parametric tests\nA: definition and scope of Parametric tests; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is t-test?\nA: comparison of means using one-sample, independent-samples or paired-samples variants\nQ: Key points for t-test\nA: compare means; independent vs paired; t statistic\nQ: What is Non-parametric tests?\nA: tests with fewer distributional assumptions, often useful for ordinal/rank data\nQ: Key points for Non-parametric tests\nA: rank-based methods; fewer assumptions; small/non-normal samples\nQ: What is Sign test?\nA: non-parametric test using only the direction of paired differences\nQ: Key points for Sign test\nA: paired data; plus/minus direction; binomial logic",
