          "flashcards": "Q: What is Assumptions of factor analysis?\nA: identification of latent dimensions underlying correlations among observed variables\nQ: Key points for Assumptions of factor analysis\nA: factors; loadings; common variance\nQ: What is Methods of factor analysis?\nA: identification of latent dimensions underlying correlations among observed variables\nQ: Key points for Methods of factor analysis\nA: factors; loadings; common variance\nQ: What is Rotation?\nA: re-expression of extracted factors to make loading patterns easier to interpret\nQ: Key points for Rotation\nA: orthogonal; oblique; interpretability\nQ: What is Interpretation?\nA: Interpretation is a focused concept within “Factor analysis: Assumptions, Methods, Rotation and Interpretation”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Interpretation\nA: definition and scope of Interpretation; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application",
          "references": "",
          "detailed_notes": "Organize factor analysis into assumptions → extraction/method → rotation → interpretation. Learn how observed variables are represented through underlying factors.\n\nATTACHED BOOK CONNECTION\nResearch Methods in Psychology: The book presents the scientific method as a systematic way of asking questions, forming hypotheses, collecting evidence and evaluating conclusions. It covers naturalistic observation, clinical and case-study methods, surveys, psychological testing, correlational research and experiments, including the roles of samples, populations, independent and dependent variables, control groups, random processes, reliability and validity. Correlation is treated as a relationship between variables rather than proof of causation.",
