          "mcqs": "Q1. Which option is most directly associated with “Item writing”?\nA. None of the above\nB. Item writing\nC. Item analysis\nD. None of the above\nAnswer: Item writing\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ2. Which option is most directly associated with “Item analysis”?\nA. None of the above\nB. Item analysis\nC. Item writing\nD. None of the above\nAnswer: Item analysis\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.",
          "flashcards": "Q: What is Item writing?\nA: Item writing is a focused concept within “Test construction: Item writing, item analysis”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Item writing\nA: definition and scope of Item writing; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Item analysis?\nA: Item analysis is a focused concept within “Test construction: Item writing, item analysis”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Item analysis\nA: definition and scope of Item analysis; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application",
          "references": "",
          "detailed_notes": "Study the sequence from defining the construct and writing items to item analysis and selection of effective items.\n\nATTACHED BOOK CONNECTION\nResearch Methods in Psychology: The book presents the scientific method as a systematic way of asking questions, forming hypotheses, collecting evidence and evaluating conclusions. It covers naturalistic observation, clinical and case-study methods, surveys, psychological testing, correlational research and experiments, including the roles of samples, populations, independent and dependent variables, control groups, random processes, reliability and validity. Correlation is treated as a relationship between variables rather than proof of causation.",
