          "mcqs": "Q1. Which option is most directly associated with “Standardization”?\nA. Norms\nB. Standardization\nC. Reliability\nD. Validity\nAnswer: Standardization\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ2. Which option is most directly associated with “Reliability”?\nA. Validity\nB. Norms\nC. Reliability\nD. Standardization\nAnswer: Reliability\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ3. Which option is most directly associated with “Validity”?\nA. Reliability\nB. Norms\nC. Validity\nD. Standardization\nAnswer: Validity\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.",
          "flashcards": "Q: What is Standardization?\nA: Standardization is a focused concept within “Test standardization: Reliability, validity and Norms”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Standardization\nA: definition and scope of Standardization; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Reliability?\nA: consistency or dependability of measurement\nQ: Key points for Reliability\nA: test-retest; internal consistency; inter-rater; split-half\nQ: What is Validity?\nA: evidence supporting the intended interpretation and use of test scores\nQ: Key points for Validity\nA: content; criterion-related; construct\nQ: What is Norms?\nA: reference information for interpreting scores relative to a defined population\nQ: Key points for Norms\nA: percentiles; age/grade norms; standard scores",
          "references": "",
          "detailed_notes": "Organize standardization around consistent administration/scoring, reliability, validity and norms. Distinguish reliability from validity.\n\nATTACHED BOOK CONNECTION\nResearch Methods in Psychology: The book presents the scientific method as a systematic way of asking questions, forming hypotheses, collecting evidence and evaluating conclusions. It covers naturalistic observation, clinical and case-study methods, surveys, psychological testing, correlational research and experiments, including the roles of samples, populations, independent and dependent variables, control groups, random processes, reliability and validity. Correlation is treated as a relationship between variables rather than proof of causation.\nIntelligence: The intelligence chapter defines intelligence in terms of effective thinking and functioning and examines how it is measured. It covers the Stanford-Binet and Wechsler approaches, mental and chronological age, IQ, verbal and performance abilities, and the concepts of reliability and validity. It also discusses factor-analytic approaches, general and specific abilities, multiple intelligences, and the continuing nature-nurture issue in intellectual development.",
          "source_scope": "Authoritative UGC NET Psychology syllabus + Prompts for notes.docx + Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide. Generated practice is PYQ-style; attached-book self-test items are source-based.",
          "bookmarks": false,
