          "title": "Attitude Scales and Computer-Based Testing",
          "explanation": "Compare the three named attitude scaling formats, then study how psychological testing can be delivered through computers.",
          "notes": "ACADEMIC CORE\nCompare the three named attitude scaling formats, then study how psychological testing can be delivered through computers.\n\nMICRO-TOPIC MAP\nSemantic differential scale • Staples scale • Likert scale • Computer-based psychological testing\n\nSOURCE BASIS\nThe topic is retained within the uploaded NET syllabus/material framework. Use the listed source books/notes below for the detailed treatment; the website should not substitute generic explanations where the source is silent.\n\nEXAM USE\nLearn the definitions, named theorists/models, classifications, mechanisms, distinctions and source-specific examples actually present in the material.",
          "status": "Not started",
          "pyqs": "PYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 1\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Semantic differential scale”?\nA. Likert scale\nB. Computer-based psychological testing\nC. Semantic differential scale\nD. Staples scale\nAnswer: Semantic differential scale\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.\n\nPYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 2\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Staples scale”?\nA. Semantic differential scale\nB. Likert scale\nC. Computer-based psychological testing\nD. Staples scale\nAnswer: Staples scale\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.",
          "mcqs": "Q1. Which option is most directly associated with “Semantic differential scale”?\nA. Staples scale\nB. Likert scale\nC. Computer-based psychological testing\nD. Semantic differential scale\nAnswer: Semantic differential scale\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ2. Which option is most directly associated with “Staples scale”?\nA. Staples scale\nB. Semantic differential scale\nC. Likert scale\nD. Computer-based psychological testing\nAnswer: Staples scale\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ3. Which option is most directly associated with “Likert scale”?\nA. Computer-based psychological testing\nB. Likert scale\nC. Semantic differential scale\nD. Staples scale\nAnswer: Likert scale\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.",
          "flashcards": "Q: What is Semantic differential scale?\nA: measurement of connotative meaning using bipolar adjective dimensions\nQ: Key points for Semantic differential scale\nA: bipolar adjectives; Osgood; attitude/meaning\nQ: What is Staples scale?\nA: Staples scale is a focused concept within “Attitude scales – Semantic differential, Staples, Likert scale. Computer-based psychological testing”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Staples scale\nA: definition and scope of Staples scale; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Likert scale?\nA: summated agreement scale using ordered response categories\nQ: Key points for Likert scale\nA: strongly agree/disagree; ordered categories; summated ratings\nQ: What is Computer-based psychological testing?\nA: Computer-based psychological testing is a focused concept within “Attitude scales – Semantic differential, Staples, Likert scale. Computer-based psychological testing”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Computer-based psychological testing\nA: definition and scope of Computer-based psychological testing; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application",
          "references": "",
          "detailed_notes": "Compare the three named attitude scaling formats, then study how psychological testing can be delivered through computers.\n\nATTACHED BOOK CONNECTION\nSocial Psychology: The social psychology chapter studies how interactions with other people influence thoughts, feelings and actions. It covers interpersonal attraction, attitudes and persuasion, social perception and attribution, conformity and social influence, cognitive dissonance, prosocial behavior, aggression, group processes and leadership. The chapter also introduces social influence experiments and the role of situational and interpersonal factors in behavior.",
