          "title": "Neurons and Neural Transmission",
          "explanation": "Learn neuron structure and function, types of neurons, generation/propagation of neural impulses and synaptic transmission.",
          "notes": "ACADEMIC CORE\nLearn neuron structure and function, types of neurons, generation/propagation of neural impulses and synaptic transmission.\n\nMICRO-TOPIC MAP\nNeuron structure • Neuron functions • Types of neurons • Neural impulse • Synaptic transmission\n\nSOURCE BASIS\nThe topic is retained within the uploaded NET syllabus/material framework. Use the listed source books/notes below for the detailed treatment; the website should not substitute generic explanations where the source is silent.\n\nEXAM USE\nLearn the definitions, named theorists/models, classifications, mechanisms, distinctions and source-specific examples actually present in the material.",
          "status": "Not started",
          "pyqs": "PYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 1\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Neuron structure”?\nA. Neuron functions\nB. Types of neurons\nC. Neural impulse\nD. Neuron structure\nAnswer: Neuron structure\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.\n\nPYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 2\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Neuron functions”?\nA. Neuron structure\nB. Types of neurons\nC. Neural impulse\nD. Neuron functions\nAnswer: Neuron functions\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.",
          "mcqs": "Q1. Which option is most directly associated with “Neuron structure”?\nA. Neuron structure\nB. Neuron functions\nC. Types of neurons\nD. Neural impulse\nAnswer: Neuron structure\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ2. Which option is most directly associated with “Neuron functions”?\nA. Neuron functions\nB. Neuron structure\nC. Types of neurons\nD. Neural impulse\nAnswer: Neuron functions\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ3. Which option is most directly associated with “Types of neurons”?\nA. Types of neurons\nB. Neuron structure\nC. Neuron functions\nD. Neural impulse\nAnswer: Types of neurons\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 3: Biology of Behavior\nQ1. An axon\na. can be found in the cell body of a neuron\nb. is a motor neuron\nc. sends information in the direction of the cell body\nd. sends information away from the cell body\nSource answer: D\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 3: Biology of Behavior\nQ2. Which one of the following is said to be a chemical messenger?\na. A cell nucleus\nb. A neurotransmitter\nc. A metabolic cell\nd. A connector neuron\nSource answer: B\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 3: Biology of Behavior\nQ3. Informally, a neuron is said to “fire.” The formal term for “fire” is\na. signal recentering\nb. depolarize\nc. repolarize\nd. adaptation\nSource answer: B\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 3: Biology of Behavior\nQ4. The two main divisions of the nervous system are\na. the brain and the spinal cord\nb. the somatic and the parasympathetic divisions\nc. the central nervous system and the peripheral nervous system\nd. the right hemisphere and the left hemisphere\nSource answer: C\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 3: Biology of Behavior\nQ5. What structure functions like both a light switch and a rheostat?\na. The medulla\nb. The thalamus\nc. The reticular activating system\nd. The cerebellum\nSource answer: C\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 3: Biology of Behavior\nQ6. The hypothalamus is associated primarily with\na. the regulation of biological drives\nb. abstract intelligence\nc. the curiosity drive\nd. motor coordination\nSource answer: A\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 3: Biology of Behavior\nQ7. The cerebral cortex is associated primarily with\na. the inhibition of respiration\nb. the regulation of biological drives\nc. primal impulses\nd. thinking\nSource answer: D\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 3: Biology of Behavior\nQ8. The right hemisphere of the brain tends to mediate\na. nonverbal patterning\nb. verbal thinking\nc. mathematical thinking\nd. symbolic logic\nSource answer: A\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 3: Biology of Behavior\nQ9. What hormone is associated with the pineal gland?\na. Thyroxin\nb. Melatonin\nc. Growth hormone\nd. Insulin\nSource answer: B\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 3: Biology of Behavior\nQ10. The ovaries belong to what larger general category of endocrine glands?\na. Stress glands\nb. Adrenal glands\nc. Gonads\nd. Pituitary glands\nSource answer: C\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).",
          "flashcards": "Q: What is Neuron structure?\nA: organization of dendrites, soma, axon and terminal regions for receiving and transmitting information\nQ: Key points for Neuron structure\nA: dendrites receive; soma integrates; axon conducts; terminals communicate\nQ: What is Neuron functions?\nA: Neuron functions is a focused concept within “Neurons: Structure, functions, types, neural impulse, synaptic transmission”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Neuron functions\nA: definition and scope of Neuron functions; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Types of neurons?\nA: functional classification into sensory, motor and interneurons\nQ: Key points for Types of neurons\nA: afferent/sensory; efferent/motor; interneurons\nQ: What is Neural impulse?\nA: action potential as a rapid, all-or-none change in membrane potential\nQ: Key points for Neural impulse\nA: threshold; depolarization; repolarization; all-or-none",
          "references": "",
          "detailed_notes": "Learn neuron structure and function, types of neurons, generation/propagation of neural impulses and synaptic transmission.\n\nATTACHED BOOK CONNECTION\nBiology of Behavior: The biology chapter explains how the nervous and endocrine systems contribute to behavior. It covers neuron structure and communication, neural impulses, synaptic transmission and neurotransmitters, and distinguishes central and peripheral nervous-system functions. It also introduces major brain structures, the autonomic nervous system, endocrine glands and biological influences on behavior such as arousal, stress, sleep and reproduction.",
