          "pyqs": "PYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 1\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Central nervous system”?\nA. Peripheral nervous system\nB. Structure and functions\nC. None of the above\nD. Central nervous system\nAnswer: Central nervous system\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.\n\nPYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 2\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Peripheral nervous system”?\nA. Peripheral nervous system\nB. Central nervous system\nC. Structure and functions\nD. None of the above\nAnswer: Peripheral nervous system\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.",
          "mcqs": "Q1. Which option is most directly associated with “Central nervous system”?\nA. Central nervous system\nB. Peripheral nervous system\nC. Structure and functions\nD. None of the above\nAnswer: Central nervous system\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ2. Which option is most directly associated with “Peripheral nervous system”?\nA. None of the above\nB. Peripheral nervous system\nC. Central nervous system\nD. Structure and functions\nAnswer: Peripheral nervous system\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ3. Which option is most directly associated with “Structure and functions”?\nA. Central nervous system\nB. Peripheral nervous system\nC. None of the above\nD. Structure and functions\nAnswer: Structure and functions\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.",
          "flashcards": "Q: What is Central nervous system?\nA: brain and spinal cord as the major integrative center\nQ: Key points for Central nervous system\nA: brain; spinal cord; integration\nQ: What is Peripheral nervous system?\nA: neural structures outside brain/spinal cord connecting CNS with the body\nQ: Key points for Peripheral nervous system\nA: somatic; autonomic; afferent/efferent\nQ: What is Structure and functions?\nA: Structure and functions is a focused concept within “The Central and Peripheral Nervous Systems – Structure and functions”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Structure and functions\nA: definition and scope of Structure and functions; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application",
          "references": "",
          "detailed_notes": "Compare CNS and PNS structurally and functionally, including their major divisions.\n\nATTACHED BOOK CONNECTION\nBiology of Behavior: The biology chapter explains how the nervous and endocrine systems contribute to behavior. It covers neuron structure and communication, neural impulses, synaptic transmission and neurotransmitters, and distinguishes central and peripheral nervous-system functions. It also introduces major brain structures, the autonomic nervous system, endocrine glands and biological influences on behavior such as arousal, stress, sleep and reproduction.",
