          "flashcards": "Q: What is Anatomical methods?\nA: Anatomical methods is a focused concept within “Methods of Physiological Psychology: Invasive methods – Anatomical methods, degeneration techniques, lesion techniques, chemical methods, microelectrode studies. Non-invasive methods – EEG, Scanning methods”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Anatomical methods\nA: definition and scope of Anatomical methods; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Degeneration techniques?\nA: Degeneration techniques is a focused concept within “Methods of Physiological Psychology: Invasive methods – Anatomical methods, degeneration techniques, lesion techniques, chemical methods, microelectrode studies. Non-invasive methods – EEG, Scanning methods”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Degeneration techniques\nA: definition and scope of Degeneration techniques; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Lesion techniques?\nA: Lesion techniques is a focused concept within “Methods of Physiological Psychology: Invasive methods – Anatomical methods, degeneration techniques, lesion techniques, chemical methods, microelectrode studies. Non-invasive methods – EEG, Scanning methods”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Lesion techniques\nA: definition and scope of Lesion techniques; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Chemical methods?\nA: Chemical methods is a focused concept within “Methods of Physiological Psychology: Invasive methods – Anatomical methods, degeneration techniques, lesion techniques, chemical methods, microelectrode studies. Non-invasive methods – EEG, Scanning methods”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Chemical methods\nA: definition and scope of Chemical methods; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application",
