          "notes": "ACADEMIC CORE\nUse this as the umbrella topic for the perception subtopics that follow: organization, constancy, depth, movement and contextual influences.\n\nMICRO-TOPIC MAP\nPerception\n\nSOURCE BASIS\nThe topic is retained within the uploaded NET syllabus/material framework. Use the listed source books/notes below for the detailed treatment; the website should not substitute generic explanations where the source is silent.\n\nEXAM USE\nLearn the definitions, named theorists/models, classifications, mechanisms, distinctions and source-specific examples actually present in the material.",
          "status": "Not started",
          "pyqs": "PYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 1\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Perception”?\nA. None of the above\nB. None of the above\nC. Perception\nD. None of the above\nAnswer: Perception\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.",
          "mcqs": "Q1. Which option is most directly associated with “Perception”?\nA. None of the above\nB. None of the above\nC. None of the above\nD. Perception\nAnswer: Perception\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 4: Sensation\nQ1. Sensation refers to\na. organized experience\nb. thinking and concept formation\nc. meaningful knowledge\nd. the raw data of experience\nSource answer: D\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 4: Sensation\nQ2. The waves to which we give the name “light” are a narrow band of\na. the electromagnetic spectrum\nb. radio waves\nc. ultra-violet waves\nd. infra-red waves\nSource answer: A\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 4: Sensation\nQ3. The trichromatic theory proposes that we have\na. three kinds of optic nerves\nb. a triad of lenses\nc. three kinds of cones\nd. three kinds of rods\nSource answer: C\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 4: Sensation\nQ4. A sound wave\na. can travel through outer space\nb. cannot travel through water\nc. has frequency, but not amplitude\nd. requires a medium such as air or water\nSource answer: D\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 4: Sensation\nQ5. What structure in the ear is similar in function to the eye’s retina?\na. The basilar membrane\nb. The auditory nerve\nc. The tympanic membrane\nd. The oval window\nSource answer: A\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 4: Sensation\nQ6. The four basic taste sensations are\na. sweet, salty, bitter, and hot\nb. sweet, bitter, burned, and salty\nc. sour, acid, sweet, and mint\nd. sweet, salty, bitter, and sour\nSource answer: D\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 4: Sensation\nQ7. One of the following is not a skin sense.\na. Light touch\nb. Incongruent pleasure\nc. Deep touch\nd. Temperature\nSource answer: B\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 4: Sensation\nQ8. The receptor organ that makes smell possible is called the\na. vestibular membrane\nb. olfactory epithelium\nc. odor membrane\nd. synaptic epithelium\nSource answer: B\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 4: Sensation\nQ9. What sense makes it possible for you to touch the tip of your nose with your eyes closed?\na. The vestibular sense\nb. The cardiovascular sense\nc. Kinesthesis\nd. Synthesis\nSource answer: C\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 4: Sensation\nQ10. The vestibular sense lets you know when\na. a signal is present\nb. a figure is perceived against a ground\nc. you are walking upright\nd. you have a subliminal perception\nSource answer: C\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).",
          "flashcards": "Q: What is Perception?\nA: Perception is a focused concept within “Perception”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Perception\nA: definition and scope of Perception; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application",
          "references": "",
          "detailed_notes": "Use this as the umbrella topic for the perception subtopics that follow: organization, constancy, depth, movement and contextual influences.\n\nATTACHED BOOK CONNECTION\nPerception: The perception chapter examines how sensory information becomes an organized psychological experience. It covers the distinction between the geographical and psychological worlds, Gestalt organization and figure-ground perception, perceptual learning, constancies and illusions, and binocular and monocular depth cues. It also discusses movement perception and the status of unusual perceptual claims such as psi powers within scientific psychology.",
