          "explanation": "Build a clear understanding of Learning Process, then connect its key ideas, distinctions, and applications.",
          "notes": "ACADEMIC CORE\nBuild a clear understanding of Learning Process, then connect its key ideas, distinctions, and applications.\n\nMICRO-TOPIC MAP\nLearning process\n\nSOURCE BASIS\nThe topic is retained within the uploaded NET syllabus/material framework. Use the listed source books/notes below for the detailed treatment; the website should not substitute generic explanations where the source is silent.\n\nEXAM USE\nLearn the definitions, named theorists/models, classifications, mechanisms, distinctions and source-specific examples actually present in the material.",
          "status": "Not started",
          "pyqs": "PYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 1\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Learning process”?\nA. Learning process\nB. None of the above\nC. None of the above\nD. None of the above\nAnswer: Learning process\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.",
          "mcqs": "Q1. Which option is most directly associated with “Learning process”?\nA. None of the above\nB. Learning process\nC. None of the above\nD. None of the above\nAnswer: Learning process\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.",
          "flashcards": "Q: What is Learning process?\nA: Learning process is a focused concept within “Learning Process”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Learning process\nA: definition and scope of Learning process; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application",
          "references": "",
          "detailed_notes": "\n\nATTACHED BOOK CONNECTION\nLearning: The learning chapter describes learning as a relatively enduring change in behavior or behavioral potential associated with experience. It covers classical conditioning, including conditioned and unconditioned stimuli and responses, extinction, discrimination and generalization; operant conditioning and reinforcement; Thorndike's law of effect; observational learning and vicarious reinforcement; insight and learning sets; and the relationship between learning and memory. Memory processes include encoding, storage and retrieval.",
          "source_scope": "Authoritative UGC NET Psychology syllabus + Prompts for notes.docx + Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide. Generated practice is PYQ-style; attached-book self-test items are source-based.",
          "bookmarks": false,
