              "title": "Instrumental learning paradigms",
              "content_notes": "At the centre of Instrumental learning paradigms is a simple idea: Instrumental learning paradigms study how responses are acquired and maintained through consequences. The uploaded sources cover reinforcement, punishment, shaping, discrimination and schedules of reinforcement as central operant procedures.\n\nThe important features are:\n• Reinforcement strengthens behavior.\n• Shaping reinforces successive approximations to a target response.\n• Discriminative stimuli signal reinforcement contingencies.\n• Continuous and partial schedules create different patterns of behavior.\n• SOURCE BASIS\n• Ciccarelli & White 6e — Operant Conditioning; Self-Teaching Guide — Instrumental Conditioning.\n• COMMON EXAM TRAP\n• A paradigm describes the arrangement of responses and consequences; it is not simply another name for one reinforcement schedule.\n• MEMORY CUE\n• Instrumental learning paradigms → Reinforcement strengthens behavior • Shaping reinforces successive approximations to a target response • Discriminative stimuli signal reinforcement contingencies\n\nFor a NET-style question, identify the defining feature first and then distinguish it from the closest alternatives.",
