          "mcqs": "Q1. Which option is most directly associated with “Verbal learning”?\nA. Verbal learning\nB. Discrimination learning\nC. None of the above\nD. None of the above\nAnswer: Verbal learning\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ2. Which option is most directly associated with “Discrimination learning”?\nA. Verbal learning\nB. None of the above\nC. None of the above\nD. Discrimination learning\nAnswer: Discrimination learning\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.",
          "flashcards": "Q: What is Verbal learning?\nA: Verbal learning is a focused concept within “Verbal learning and Discrimination learning”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Verbal learning\nA: definition and scope of Verbal learning; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Discrimination learning?\nA: differential treatment based on group membership or perceived group membership\nQ: Key points for Discrimination learning\nA: stereotype = belief; prejudice = evaluation; discrimination = behavior",
          "references": "",
          "detailed_notes": "Study learning involving verbal material and the acquisition of discriminations between stimuli or response categories.\n\nATTACHED BOOK CONNECTION\nLearning: The learning chapter describes learning as a relatively enduring change in behavior or behavioral potential associated with experience. It covers classical conditioning, including conditioned and unconditioned stimuli and responses, extinction, discrimination and generalization; operant conditioning and reinforcement; Thorndike's law of effect; observational learning and vicarious reinforcement; insight and learning sets; and the relationship between learning and memory. Memory processes include encoding, storage and retrieval.",
