              "deep_learning": "At the centre of Information processing perspective is a simple idea: The information-processing perspective explains thought as a set of processes through which information is received, represented, stored, transformed and used. The uploaded material links this perspective with attention, memory, problem solving and decision making.\n\nThese features should be learned as parts of one psychological idea rather than as disconnected facts. Mental activity is described in terms of information processing. Attention selects or prioritizes information. Memory provides stored representations used in thinking. Problem solving and decision making involve multiple processing operations. SOURCE BASIS. PowerWithin Psychology — Unit 6 Cognitive Psychology; Kaplan AP Psychology — cognition and problem solving. COMMON EXAM TRAP. Information processing is a framework for describing cognitive operations; it is not one single experiment or one single cognitive ability. MEMORY CUE. Information processing perspective → Mental activity is described in terms of information processing • Attention selects or prioritizes information • Memory provides stored representations used in thinking.\n\nSource grounding: PowerWithin Psychology — Unit 6 Cognitive Psychology; Kaplan AP Psychology — cognition and problem solving.",
