          "id": 6,
          "title": "Theories of Intelligence",
          "explanation": "Build a theorist-by-model table. The syllabus expects familiarity with each named contributor/framework.",
          "notes": "ACADEMIC CORE\nBuild a theorist-by-model table. The syllabus expects familiarity with each named contributor/framework.\n\nMICRO-TOPIC MAP\nSpearman • Thurstone • Jensen • Cattell • Gardner • Stenberg • Goleman • Das, Kar & Parrila\n\nSOURCE BASIS\nThe topic is retained within the uploaded NET syllabus/material framework. Use the listed source books/notes below for the detailed treatment; the website should not substitute generic explanations where the source is silent.\n\nEXAM USE\nLearn the definitions, named theorists/models, classifications, mechanisms, distinctions and source-specific examples actually present in the material.",
          "status": "Not started",
          "pyqs": "PYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 1\nQ. Spearman's two-factor theory includes:\nA. Analytical, creative and practical\nB. Eight independent intelligences\nC. g and s\nD. Seven primary abilities\nAnswer: g and s\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.\n\nPYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 2\nQ. Primary Mental Abilities theory was proposed by:\nA. Thurstone\nB. Gardner\nC. Guilford\nD. Spearman\nAnswer: Thurstone\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.",
          "mcqs": "Q1. Spearman's two-factor theory includes:\nA. Seven primary abilities\nB. Analytical, creative and practical\nC. Eight independent intelligences\nD. g and s\nAnswer: g and s\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ2. Primary Mental Abilities theory was proposed by:\nA. Spearman\nB. Thurstone\nC. Gardner\nD. Guilford\nAnswer: Thurstone\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ3. Which option is most directly associated with “Jensen”?\nA. Cattell\nB. Jensen\nC. Spearman\nD. Thurstone\nAnswer: Jensen\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 10: Intelligence\nQ1. One of the following is not a primary characteristic of intelligence:\na. The ability to think clearly\nb. Eccentric thinking\nc. Convergent thinking\nd. Functioning effectively in the environment\nSource answer: B\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 10: Intelligence\nQ2. In behavioral science, intelligence has the status of\na. a hypothetical construct\nb. a psychological atom\nc. a transcendental force\nd. an interim operant\nSource answer: A\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 10: Intelligence\nQ3. What method was employed by Binet and Simon to measure intelligence?\na. The biometric method\nb. The perceptual-motor method\nc. The performance method\nd. The physiological method\nSource answer: C\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 10: Intelligence\nQ4. A research project that measures behavior over a span of time is called a\na. discontinuity study\nb. gradient study\nc. longitudinal study\nd. continuity study Intelligence: In Pursuit of Rational Thought and Effective Action 151\nSource answer: C\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 10: Intelligence\nQ5. Which of the following is the correct formula for the intelligence quotient (IQ)?\na. IQ = MA × CA + 100\nb. IQ = CA + MA × 3.14\nc. IQ = MA + MA/100\nd. IQ = MA/CA × 100\nSource answer: D\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 10: Intelligence\nQ6. An IQ score in the range 110 to 119 is associated with what classification?\na. Very Superior\nb. Superior\nc. Bright Normal\nd. Normal (or Average)\nSource answer: C\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 10: Intelligence\nQ7. A test that measures what it is supposed to measure is said to be\na. consistent\nb. reliable\nc. valid\nd. congruent\nSource answer: C\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 10: Intelligence\nQ8. A test that gives stable, repeatable results is said to be\na. authentic\nb. reliable\nc. valid\nd. systematic\nSource answer: B\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 10: Intelligence\nQ9. The inborn potential to reach a given level of intelligence is associated primarily with which of the following?\na. Nature\nb. Nurture\nc. Environment\nd. Reinforcement\nSource answer: A\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 10: Intelligence\nQ10. The correct formula for understanding intelligence is which of the following?\na. Heredity × Environment = Intelligence\nb. Heredity + Environment = Intelligence\nc. Nature × Heredity = Intelligence\nd. Nurture × Environment = Intelligence\nSource answer: A\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).",
          "flashcards": "Q: What is Spearman?\nA: two-factor theory of intelligence: general factor g plus specific factors s\nQ: Key points for Spearman\nA: g; s; factor analysis\nQ: What is Thurstone?\nA: primary mental abilities approach to intelligence\nQ: Key points for Thurstone\nA: seven primary abilities; verbal comprehension; reasoning; number/space\nQ: What is Jensen?\nA: Jensen is a focused concept within “Intelligence: Spearman; Thurstone; Jensen; Cattell; Gardner; Stenberg; Goleman; Das, Kar & Parrila”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Jensen\nA: definition and scope of Jensen; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Cattell?\nA: distinction between fluid and crystallized intelligence\nQ: Key points for Cattell\nA: fluid; crystallized; developmental change",
          "references": "",
          "detailed_notes": "Build a theorist-by-model table. The syllabus expects familiarity with each named contributor/framework.\n\nATTACHED BOOK CONNECTION\nIntelligence: The intelligence chapter defines intelligence in terms of effective thinking and functioning and examines how it is measured. It covers the Stanford-Binet and Wechsler approaches, mental and chronological age, IQ, verbal and performance abilities, and the concepts of reliability and validity. It also discusses factor-analytic approaches, general and specific abilities, multiple intelligences, and the continuing nature-nurture issue in intellectual development.",
