          "explanation": "Build a parallel theorist map for the named creativity approaches and measures.",
          "notes": "ACADEMIC CORE\nBuild a parallel theorist map for the named creativity approaches and measures.\n\nMICRO-TOPIC MAP\nTorrance • Getzels & Jackson • Guilford • Wallach & Kogan\n\nSOURCE BASIS\nThe topic is retained within the uploaded NET syllabus/material framework. Use the listed source books/notes below for the detailed treatment; the website should not substitute generic explanations where the source is silent.\n\nEXAM USE\nLearn the definitions, named theorists/models, classifications, mechanisms, distinctions and source-specific examples actually present in the material.",
          "status": "Not started",
          "pyqs": "PYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 1\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Torrance”?\nA. Wallach & Kogan\nB. Torrance\nC. Getzels & Jackson\nD. Guilford\nAnswer: Torrance\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.\n\nPYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 2\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Getzels & Jackson”?\nA. Guilford\nB. Wallach & Kogan\nC. Getzels & Jackson\nD. Torrance\nAnswer: Getzels & Jackson\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.",
          "mcqs": "Q1. Which option is most directly associated with “Torrance”?\nA. Guilford\nB. Wallach & Kogan\nC. Torrance\nD. Getzels & Jackson\nAnswer: Torrance\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ2. Which option is most directly associated with “Getzels & Jackson”?\nA. Torrance\nB. Guilford\nC. Wallach & Kogan\nD. Getzels & Jackson\nAnswer: Getzels & Jackson\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ3. Which option is most directly associated with “Guilford”?\nA. Guilford\nB. Torrance\nC. Getzels & Jackson\nD. Wallach & Kogan\nAnswer: Guilford\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.",
          "flashcards": "Q: What is Torrance?\nA: creativity assessment emphasizing divergent production\nQ: Key points for Torrance\nA: fluency; flexibility; originality; elaboration\nQ: What is Getzels & Jackson?\nA: Getzels & Jackson is a focused concept within “Creativity: Torrance, Getzels & Jackson, Guilford, Wallach & Kogan”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Getzels & Jackson\nA: definition and scope of Getzels & Jackson; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Guilford?\nA: Guilford is a focused concept within “Creativity: Torrance, Getzels & Jackson, Guilford, Wallach & Kogan”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Guilford\nA: definition and scope of Guilford; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Wallach & Kogan?\nA: Wallach & Kogan is a focused concept within “Creativity: Torrance, Getzels & Jackson, Guilford, Wallach & Kogan”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Wallach & Kogan\nA: definition and scope of Wallach & Kogan; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application",
          "references": "",
          "detailed_notes": "Build a parallel theorist map for the named creativity approaches and measures.\n\nATTACHED BOOK CONNECTION\nThinking: The thinking chapter examines concepts, reasoning, problem solving, decision making and creativity. It describes concepts as mental organizations of experience and distinguishes different forms of reasoning. Problem solving is discussed in terms of strategies, mental sets and obstacles such as functional fixedness. The chapter also contrasts logical thinking with common errors and identifies divergent thinking as central to creative production.\nIntelligence: The intelligence chapter defines intelligence in terms of effective thinking and functioning and examines how it is measured. It covers the Stanford-Binet and Wechsler approaches, mental and chronological age, IQ, verbal and performance abilities, and the concepts of reliability and validity. It also discusses factor-analytic approaches, general and specific abilities, multiple intelligences, and the continuing nature-nurture issue in intellectual development.",
