              "title": "Wallach & Kogan",
              "content_notes": "At the centre of Wallach & Kogan is a simple idea: Wallach and Kogan are included in the uploaded NET material among major creativity theorists. Their work is relevant to the distinction between creativity and conventional intelligence testing and to the use of more open-ended methods for assessing creative thinking.\n\nThe important features are:\n• Wallach and Kogan are associated with creativity assessment.\n• Their work is used in discussions distinguishing creativity from conventional intelligence.\n• Open-ended performance can be important when assessing creative production.\n• The source places them within the creativity-theory sequence.\n• SOURCE BASIS\n• PowerWithin Psychology — Unit 6 Theories of Creativity; Wallach & Kogan coverage.\n• COMMON EXAM TRAP\n• Do not substitute Guilford's Structure of Intellect model for Wallach and Kogan's specific contribution.\n• MEMORY CUE\n• Wallach & Kogan → Wallach and Kogan are associated with creativity assessment • Their work is used in discussions distinguishing creativity from conventional intelligence • Open-ended performance can be important when assessing creative production\n\nFor a NET-style question, identify the defining feature first and then distinguish it from the closest alternatives.",
