          "explanation": "Create a concept chain and distinguish the six motivational terms.",
          "notes": "ACADEMIC CORE\nCreate a concept chain and distinguish the six motivational terms.\n\nMICRO-TOPIC MAP\nInstincts • Needs • Drives • Arousal • Incentives • Motivational cycle\n\nSOURCE BASIS\nThe topic is retained within the uploaded NET syllabus/material framework. Use the listed source books/notes below for the detailed treatment; the website should not substitute generic explanations where the source is silent.\n\nEXAM USE\nLearn the definitions, named theorists/models, classifications, mechanisms, distinctions and source-specific examples actually present in the material.",
          "status": "Not started",
          "pyqs": "PYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 1\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Instincts”?\nA. Instincts\nB. Needs\nC. Drives\nD. Arousal\nAnswer: Instincts\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.\n\nPYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 2\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Needs”?\nA. Needs\nB. Instincts\nC. Drives\nD. Arousal\nAnswer: Needs\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.",
          "mcqs": "Q1. Which option is most directly associated with “Instincts”?\nA. Arousal\nB. Instincts\nC. Needs\nD. Drives\nAnswer: Instincts\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ2. Which option is most directly associated with “Needs”?\nA. Arousal\nB. Needs\nC. Instincts\nD. Drives\nAnswer: Needs\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ3. Which option is most directly associated with “Drives”?\nA. Instincts\nB. Needs\nC. Arousal\nD. Drives\nAnswer: Drives\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 7: Motivation\nQ1. From the point of view of psychology as a science a motive is\na. a dependent variable\nb. an independent variable\nc. a radical variable\nd. an intervening variable\nSource answer: D\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 7: Motivation\nQ2. A physiological process characterized by a tendency for biological drives to maintain themselves at optimal levels of arousal is called\na. homeostasis\nb. metamotivation\nc. hyperstatic integration\nd. heterostasis\nSource answer: A\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 7: Motivation\nQ3. Which of the following is clearly associated with the curiosity drive?\na. The need to escape from pain\nb. The need for affiliation\nc. The search for meaning in life\nd. The tendency to seek novel stimulation\nSource answer: D\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 7: Motivation\nQ4. Which of the following is a motive to associate with others?\na. The need for dominance\nb. The need for exhibition\nc. The need for aggression\nd. The need for affiliation\nSource answer: D\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 7: Motivation\nQ5. According to Freud, what force in the mind is responsible for the creation of unconscious motives?\na. Repression\nb. Ego inhibition\nc. Superego excitation\nd. Homeostasis\nSource answer: A\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 7: Motivation\nQ6. Which one of the following is associated with cognitive needs?\na. Seeking a higher rank within an organization\nb. Working a crossword puzzle\nc. Looking for love\nd. Searching for shelter\nSource answer: B\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 7: Motivation\nQ7. Self-actualization is most closely linked to which of the following?\na. Feeling hungry\nb. Maximizing potentialities\nc. Seeking novel stimulation\nd. Wanting affection\nSource answer: B\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 7: Motivation\nQ8. What does Maslow call moments of joy or ecstasy experienced when a hurdle is overcome, a task is completed, or a goal is reached?\na. Hedonic experiences\nb. Transcendental experiences\nc. Peak experiences\nd. Summit experiences\nSource answer: C\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 7: Motivation\nQ9. Frankl argues that the highest level of motivation for human beings is the\na. will to meaning\nb. need for transcendental experience\nc. wish to become one with the All\nd. desire to exercise the will to power Motivation: Why Do We Do What We Do? 105\nSource answer: A\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 7: Motivation\nQ10. If a person lives a meaningless life, then that life will be empty and pointless. Frankl calls this mental and emotional state\na. major depressive episode\nb. bipolar disorder\nc. the existential vacuum\nd. biochemical depression\nSource answer: C\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).",
          "flashcards": "Q: What is Instincts?\nA: Instincts is a focused concept within “Basic motivational concepts: Instincts, Needs, Drives, Arousal, Incentives, Motivational Cycle”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Instincts\nA: definition and scope of Instincts; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Needs?\nA: Needs is a focused concept within “Basic motivational concepts: Instincts, Needs, Drives, Arousal, Incentives, Motivational Cycle”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Needs\nA: definition and scope of Needs; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Drives?\nA: Drives is a focused concept within “Basic motivational concepts: Instincts, Needs, Drives, Arousal, Incentives, Motivational Cycle”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Drives\nA: definition and scope of Drives; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Arousal?\nA: Arousal is a focused concept within “Basic motivational concepts: Instincts, Needs, Drives, Arousal, Incentives, Motivational Cycle”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Arousal\nA: definition and scope of Arousal; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application",
          "references": "",
          "detailed_notes": "Create a concept chain and distinguish the six motivational terms.\n\nATTACHED BOOK CONNECTION\nMotivation: The motivation chapter treats motives as forces behind action and explains their interaction with learning. It distinguishes biological drives from general and acquired motives, discusses unconscious motivation, and examines needs such as autonomy, achievement and related motives. The chapter also introduces Maslow's self-actualization and Frankl's emphasis on meaning, linking motivation with broader questions about purpose and human potential.",
          "source_scope": "Authoritative UGC NET Psychology syllabus + Prompts for notes.docx + Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide. Generated practice is PYQ-style; attached-book self-test items are source-based.",
