          "notes": "ACADEMIC CORE\nCompare the four named approaches by their proposed sources and mechanisms of motivation.\n\nMICRO-TOPIC MAP\nPsychoanalytical approach • Ethological approach • S-R Cognitive approach • Humanistic approach\n\nSOURCE BASIS\nThe topic is retained within the uploaded NET syllabus/material framework. Use the listed source books/notes below for the detailed treatment; the website should not substitute generic explanations where the source is silent.\n\nEXAM USE\nLearn the definitions, named theorists/models, classifications, mechanisms, distinctions and source-specific examples actually present in the material.",
          "status": "Not started",
          "pyqs": "PYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 1\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Psychoanalytical approach”?\nA. Humanistic approach\nB. Psychoanalytical approach\nC. Ethological approach\nD. S-R Cognitive approach\nAnswer: Psychoanalytical approach\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.\n\nPYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 2\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Ethological approach”?\nA. S-R Cognitive approach\nB. Humanistic approach\nC. Ethological approach\nD. Psychoanalytical approach\nAnswer: Ethological approach\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.",
          "mcqs": "Q1. Which option is most directly associated with “Psychoanalytical approach”?\nA. S-R Cognitive approach\nB. Humanistic approach\nC. Psychoanalytical approach\nD. Ethological approach\nAnswer: Psychoanalytical approach\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ2. Which option is most directly associated with “Ethological approach”?\nA. Psychoanalytical approach\nB. S-R Cognitive approach\nC. Humanistic approach\nD. Ethological approach\nAnswer: Ethological approach\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ3. Which option is most directly associated with “S-R Cognitive approach”?\nA. S-R Cognitive approach\nB. Psychoanalytical approach\nC. Ethological approach\nD. Humanistic approach\nAnswer: S-R Cognitive approach\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.",
          "flashcards": "Q: What is Psychoanalytical approach?\nA: Psychoanalytical approach is a focused concept within “Approaches to the study of motivation: Psychoanalytical, Ethological, S-R Cognitive, Humanistic”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Psychoanalytical approach\nA: definition and scope of Psychoanalytical approach; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Ethological approach?\nA: Ethological approach is a focused concept within “Approaches to the study of motivation: Psychoanalytical, Ethological, S-R Cognitive, Humanistic”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Ethological approach\nA: definition and scope of Ethological approach; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is S-R Cognitive approach?\nA: S-R Cognitive approach is a focused concept within “Approaches to the study of motivation: Psychoanalytical, Ethological, S-R Cognitive, Humanistic”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for S-R Cognitive approach\nA: definition and scope of S-R Cognitive approach; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Humanistic approach?\nA: Humanistic approach is a focused concept within “Approaches to the study of motivation: Psychoanalytical, Ethological, S-R Cognitive, Humanistic”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Humanistic approach\nA: definition and scope of Humanistic approach; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application",
          "references": "",
          "detailed_notes": "Compare the four named approaches by their proposed sources and mechanisms of motivation.\n\nATTACHED BOOK CONNECTION\nMotivation: The motivation chapter treats motives as forces behind action and explains their interaction with learning. It distinguishes biological drives from general and acquired motives, discusses unconscious motivation, and examines needs such as autonomy, achievement and related motives. The chapter also introduces Maslow's self-actualization and Frankl's emphasis on meaning, linking motivation with broader questions about purpose and human potential.",
