          "flashcards": "Q: What is Field theory?\nA: Field theory is a focused concept within “Traditional theoretical perspectives: Field theory, Cognitive Dissonance, Sociobiology, Psychodynamic Approaches, Social Cognition”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Field theory\nA: definition and scope of Field theory; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Cognitive Dissonance?\nA: discomfort arising from inconsistency among cognitions or between cognition and behavior\nQ: Key points for Cognitive Dissonance\nA: Festinger; inconsistency; dissonance reduction\nQ: What is Sociobiology?\nA: Sociobiology is a focused concept within “Traditional theoretical perspectives: Field theory, Cognitive Dissonance, Sociobiology, Psychodynamic Approaches, Social Cognition”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Sociobiology\nA: definition and scope of Sociobiology; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Psychodynamic approaches?\nA: Psychodynamic approaches is a focused concept within “Traditional theoretical perspectives: Field theory, Cognitive Dissonance, Sociobiology, Psychodynamic Approaches, Social Cognition”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Psychodynamic approaches\nA: definition and scope of Psychodynamic approaches; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application",
          "references": "",
          "detailed_notes": "Compare the five perspectives by their explanation of social behaviour and the constructs they emphasize.\n\nATTACHED BOOK CONNECTION\nSocial Psychology: The social psychology chapter studies how interactions with other people influence thoughts, feelings and actions. It covers interpersonal attraction, attitudes and persuasion, social perception and attribution, conformity and social influence, cognitive dissonance, prosocial behavior, aggression, group processes and leadership. The chapter also introduces social influence experiments and the role of situational and interpersonal factors in behavior.",
