              "id": 4,
              "title": "Attitude",
              "content_notes": "At the centre of Attitude is a simple idea: An attitude is an evaluative tendency toward a person, object, issue or behavior. The uploaded sources treat attitudes as important social-psychological constructs that can influence behavior and can themselves be changed by experience and persuasion.\n\nThe important features are:\n• Attitudes have an evaluative component.\n• They can be positive, negative or mixed.\n• Attitudes can influence behavior under appropriate conditions.\n• Attitudes are shaped by social experience and information.\n• SOURCE BASIS\n• Baron & Misra — Social Psychology; PowerWithin Psychology — Attitude and Attitude Change.\n• COMMON EXAM TRAP\n• An attitude is an evaluation, not simply knowledge or a belief; beliefs may contribute to an attitude.\n• MEMORY CUE\n• Attitude → Attitudes have an evaluative component • They can be positive, negative or mixed • Attitudes can influence behavior under appropriate conditions\n\nFor a NET-style question, identify the defining feature first and then distinguish it from the closest alternatives.",
