          "flashcards": "Q: What is Minimal Group Experiment?\nA: Minimal Group Experiment is a focused concept within “Theories of intergroup relations: Minimal Group Experiment and Social Identity Theory, Relative Deprivation Theory, Realistic Conflict Theory, Balance Theories, Equity Theory, Social Exchange Theory”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Minimal Group Experiment\nA: definition and scope of Minimal Group Experiment; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Social Identity Theory?\nA: intergroup behavior through categorization, identification and social comparison\nQ: Key points for Social Identity Theory\nA: Tajfel & Turner; ingroup/outgroup; social comparison\nQ: What is Relative Deprivation Theory?\nA: Relative Deprivation Theory is a focused concept within “Theories of intergroup relations: Minimal Group Experiment and Social Identity Theory, Relative Deprivation Theory, Realistic Conflict Theory, Balance Theories, Equity Theory, Social Exchange Theory”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Relative Deprivation Theory\nA: definition and scope of Relative Deprivation Theory; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Realistic Conflict Theory?\nA: intergroup hostility partly through competition over scarce resources\nQ: Key points for Realistic Conflict Theory\nA: Sherif; competition; superordinate goals",
