              "id": 3,
              "title": "Relative Deprivation Theory",
              "content_notes": "At the centre of Relative Deprivation Theory is a simple idea: Relative deprivation concerns perceived disadvantage that arises from comparison with relevant others or standards, rather than from absolute deprivation alone. Baron & Misra discuss relative deprivation in relation to intergroup attitudes and prejudice.\n\nThe important features are:\n• Perceived comparison is central.\n• People can feel deprived even when basic needs are met.\n• Relative deprivation can influence group attitudes and conflict.\n• The reference group or standard of comparison matters.\n• SOURCE BASIS\n• Baron & Misra — Relative Deprivation and Intergroup Attitudes; PowerWithin Psychology — Intergroup Relations.\n• COMMON EXAM TRAP\n• Relative deprivation is a perception of disadvantage; it is not identical to objective poverty.\n• MEMORY CUE\n• Relative Deprivation Theory → Perceived comparison is central • People can feel deprived even when basic needs are met • Relative deprivation can influence group attitudes and conflict\n\nFor a NET-style question, identify the defining feature first and then distinguish it from the closest alternatives.",
