              "content_notes": "At the centre of Personal space is a simple idea: Personal space is the preferred physical distance people maintain around themselves during social interaction. Ciccarelli & White describes it as a form of territorial protection and notes that it can be influenced by interpersonal and cultural factors.\n\nThe important features are:\n• Personal space varies across people and situations.\n• It regulates interpersonal distance.\n• Crowding can occur when desired space is reduced.\n• Cultural context can influence norms for interpersonal distance.\n• SOURCE BASIS\n• Ciccarelli & White 6e — Personal Space; PowerWithin Psychology — Applied Social Psychology.\n• COMMON EXAM TRAP\n• Personal space is not the same as territoriality: personal space is the interpersonal distance around the person, whereas territoriality concerns control of a physical area.\n• MEMORY CUE\n• Personal space → Personal space varies across people and situations • It regulates interpersonal distance • Crowding can occur when desired space is reduced\n\nFor a NET-style question, identify the defining feature first and then distinguish it from the closest alternatives.",
