          "title": "Motivation and Learning in Schools",
          "explanation": "Study how motivational and learning principles can be translated into educational practice.",
          "notes": "ACADEMIC CORE\nStudy how motivational and learning principles can be translated into educational practice.\n\nMICRO-TOPIC MAP\nApplications of motivation theories in school • Applications of learning theories in school\n\nSOURCE BASIS\nThe topic is retained within the uploaded NET syllabus/material framework. Use the listed source books/notes below for the detailed treatment; the website should not substitute generic explanations where the source is silent.\n\nEXAM USE\nLearn the definitions, named theorists/models, classifications, mechanisms, distinctions and source-specific examples actually present in the material.",
          "status": "Not started",
          "pyqs": "PYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 1\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Applications of motivation theories in school”?\nA. Applications of learning theories in school\nB. None of the above\nC. None of the above\nD. Applications of motivation theories in school\nAnswer: Applications of motivation theories in school\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.\n\nPYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 2\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Applications of learning theories in school”?\nA. None of the above\nB. Applications of learning theories in school\nC. Applications of motivation theories in school\nD. None of the above\nAnswer: Applications of learning theories in school\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.",
          "mcqs": "Q1. Which option is most directly associated with “Applications of motivation theories in school”?\nA. Applications of motivation theories in school\nB. Applications of learning theories in school\nC. None of the above\nD. None of the above\nAnswer: Applications of motivation theories in school\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ2. Which option is most directly associated with “Applications of learning theories in school”?\nA. None of the above\nB. None of the above\nC. Applications of learning theories in school\nD. Applications of motivation theories in school\nAnswer: Applications of learning theories in school\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.",
          "flashcards": "Q: What is Applications of motivation theories in school?\nA: Applications of motivation theories in school is a focused concept within “Applications of theories of motivation and learning in School”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Applications of motivation theories in school\nA: definition and scope of Applications of motivation theories in school; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Applications of learning theories in school?\nA: Applications of learning theories in school is a focused concept within “Applications of theories of motivation and learning in School”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Applications of learning theories in school\nA: definition and scope of Applications of learning theories in school; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application",
          "references": "",
          "detailed_notes": "Study how motivational and learning principles can be translated into educational practice.\n\nATTACHED BOOK CONNECTION\nLearning: The learning chapter describes learning as a relatively enduring change in behavior or behavioral potential associated with experience. It covers classical conditioning, including conditioned and unconditioned stimuli and responses, extinction, discrimination and generalization; operant conditioning and reinforcement; Thorndike's law of effect; observational learning and vicarious reinforcement; insight and learning sets; and the relationship between learning and memory. Memory processes include encoding, storage and retrieval.\nMotivation: The motivation chapter treats motives as forces behind action and explains their interaction with learning. It distinguishes biological drives from general and acquired motives, discusses unconscious motivation, and examines needs such as autonomy, achievement and related motives. The chapter also introduces Maslow's self-actualization and Frankl's emphasis on meaning, linking motivation with broader questions about purpose and human potential.",
          "source_scope": "Authoritative UGC NET Psychology syllabus + Prompts for notes.docx + Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide. Generated practice is PYQ-style; attached-book self-test items are source-based.",
