          "flashcards": "Q: What is Needs for school guidance?\nA: Needs for school guidance is a focused concept within “Guidance in schools: Needs, organizational set up and techniques”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Needs for school guidance\nA: definition and scope of Needs for school guidance; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Organizational setup for school guidance?\nA: Organizational setup for school guidance is a focused concept within “Guidance in schools: Needs, organizational set up and techniques”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Organizational setup for school guidance\nA: definition and scope of Organizational setup for school guidance; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is School guidance techniques?\nA: School guidance techniques is a focused concept within “Guidance in schools: Needs, organizational set up and techniques”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for School guidance techniques\nA: definition and scope of School guidance techniques; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application",
          "references": "",
          "detailed_notes": "Study why schools need guidance, how guidance can be organized institutionally, and the major techniques used.\n\nATTACHED BOOK CONNECTION\nDevelopmental Psychology: The developmental chapter follows development from biological beginnings through childhood and later life. It covers reproductive and fetal development, Freud's psychosexual theory, Erikson's psychosocial stages, Piaget's cognitive stages and Kohlberg's moral-development levels. It also examines parental styles and the role of heredity and environment, showing how biological maturation and experience interact in development.\nTherapy: The therapy chapter distinguishes psychologically based and biologically based approaches to helping people with mental and emotional problems. It covers psychoanalysis and free association, client-centered therapy and unconditional positive regard, behavior therapy and operant conditioning, rational-emotive behavior therapy, cognitive-behavior therapy, group approaches and major classes of psychiatric medication. Different therapies are presented as being based on different explanations of psychological difficulty.",
