          "id": 1,
          "title": "Gender, Poverty, Disability and Migration",
          "explanation": "Study each issue through cultural bias, discrimination and the psychological/social consequences of marginalization.",
          "notes": "ACADEMIC CORE\nStudy each issue through cultural bias, discrimination and the psychological/social consequences of marginalization.\n\nMICRO-TOPIC MAP\nGender • Poverty • Disability • Migration • Cultural bias • Discrimination\n\nSOURCE BASIS\nThe topic is retained within the uploaded NET syllabus/material framework. Use the listed source books/notes below for the detailed treatment; the website should not substitute generic explanations where the source is silent.\n\nEXAM USE\nLearn the definitions, named theorists/models, classifications, mechanisms, distinctions and source-specific examples actually present in the material.",
          "status": "Not started",
          "pyqs": "PYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 1\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Gender”?\nA. Disability\nB. Migration\nC. Gender\nD. Poverty\nAnswer: Gender\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.\n\nPYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 2\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Poverty”?\nA. Disability\nB. Migration\nC. Poverty\nD. Gender\nAnswer: Poverty\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.",
          "mcqs": "Q1. Which option is most directly associated with “Gender”?\nA. Poverty\nB. Disability\nC. Migration\nD. Gender\nAnswer: Gender\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ2. Which option is most directly associated with “Poverty”?\nA. Gender\nB. Disability\nC. Migration\nD. Poverty\nAnswer: Poverty\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ3. Which option is most directly associated with “Disability”?\nA. Poverty\nB. Migration\nC. Disability\nD. Gender\nAnswer: Disability\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 12: Sex and Love\nQ1. Which of the following is not a stage of the four-stage sexual response cycle?\na. Excitement\nb. Plateau\nc. Orgasm\nd. Homeostasis\nSource answer: D\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 12: Sex and Love\nQ2. Which of the following is a correct statement?\na. Women have substantially greater multi-orgasmic capacity than do men.\nb. Men have substantially greater multi-orgasmic capacity than do women.\nc. Neither sex is capable of multiple orgasms.\nd. Freud proved that the concept of multiple orgasms is a myth.\nSource answer: A\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 12: Sex and Love\nQ3. What dysfunction exists when the female does not respond to the kind of stimulation that is otherwise expected to induce excitement?\na. Female orgasmic disorder\nb. Vaginismus\nc. Female sexual arousal disorder\nd. Sexual aversion disorder\nSource answer: C\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 12: Sex and Love\nQ4. What disorder exists when the female is seldom, or never, able to attain an orgasm during sexual activity?\na. Inhibited orgasmic disorder\nb. Frigidity\nc. Female orgasmic disorder\nd. Dysfunctional orgasmic excitation\nSource answer: C\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 12: Sex and Love\nQ5. The older term impotence is associated with what disorder?\na. Premature ejaculation\nb. Male erectile disorder\nc. Male orgasmic disorder\nd. Male resolution disorder\nSource answer: B\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 12: Sex and Love\nQ6. Male orgasmic disorder is also known as\na. excitement disorder\nb. facilitated orgasmic response\nc. transvestic inhibition\nd. retarded ejaculation\nSource answer: D\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 12: Sex and Love\nQ7. What paraphilia is characterized by the use of an inanimate object such as a stocking or a shoe as a sexual stimulus?\na. Fetishism\nb. Pedophilia\nc. Sadism\nd. Masochism\nSource answer: A\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 12: Sex and Love\nQ8. What paraphilia refers to extracting sexual pleasure from physical pain?\na. Pederasty\nb. Masochism\nc. Pedophilia\nd. Transvestism\nSource answer: B\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 12: Sex and Love\nQ9. What term refers exclusively to female homosexual behavior?\na. Gender identity disorder\nb. Transsexualism\nc. Lesbianism\nd. XX-oriented fetishism\nSource answer: C\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 12: Sex and Love\nQ10. What term does the psychologist Dorothy Tennov use to identify the distinctive pattern of thoughts and emotions associated with being in love?\na. Conjugal love\nb. Limerence\nc. Idealization\nd. Transcendental ecstasy\nSource answer: B\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).",
          "flashcards": "Q: What is Gender?\nA: social and psychological dimension shaped by identity, roles, norms and context\nQ: Key points for Gender\nA: gender identity; roles; norms; intersectionality\nQ: What is Poverty?\nA: Poverty is a focused concept within “Issues of Gender, Poverty, Disability, and Migration: Cultural bias and discrimination”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Poverty\nA: definition and scope of Poverty; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Disability?\nA: Disability is a focused concept within “Issues of Gender, Poverty, Disability, and Migration: Cultural bias and discrimination”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Disability\nA: definition and scope of Disability; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Migration?\nA: Migration is a focused concept within “Issues of Gender, Poverty, Disability, and Migration: Cultural bias and discrimination”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Migration\nA: definition and scope of Migration; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application",
          "references": "",
          "detailed_notes": "Study each issue through cultural bias, discrimination and the psychological/social consequences of marginalization.\n\nATTACHED BOOK CONNECTION\nSex and Love: The chapter covers the human sexual response cycle, sexual dysfunctions and variations, and the psychological dimensions of intimacy and romantic love. It distinguishes biological aspects of sexual behavior from psychological and interpersonal influences and discusses how cultural and relational factors shape sexual and romantic experience.",
