          "pyqs": "PYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 1\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Health-promoting behaviors”?\nA. Lifestyle\nB. Diabetes\nC. Health-promoting behaviors\nD. Health-compromising behaviors\nAnswer: Health-promoting behaviors\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.\n\nPYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 2\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Health-compromising behaviors”?\nA. Lifestyle\nB. Diabetes\nC. Health-compromising behaviors\nD. Health-promoting behaviors\nAnswer: Health-compromising behaviors\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.",
          "mcqs": "Q1. Which option is most directly associated with “Health-promoting behaviors”?\nA. Health-compromising behaviors\nB. Lifestyle\nC. Diabetes\nD. Health-promoting behaviors\nAnswer: Health-promoting behaviors\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ2. Which option is most directly associated with “Health-compromising behaviors”?\nA. Health-promoting behaviors\nB. Lifestyle\nC. Diabetes\nD. Health-compromising behaviors\nAnswer: Health-compromising behaviors\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ3. Which option is most directly associated with “Lifestyle”?\nA. Health-promoting behaviors\nB. Health-compromising behaviors\nC. Diabetes\nD. Lifestyle\nAnswer: Lifestyle\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.",
          "flashcards": "Q: What is Health-promoting behaviors?\nA: Health-promoting behaviors is a focused concept within “Health: Health promoting and health compromising behaviors, Life style and Chronic diseases (Diabetes, Hypertension, Coronary Heart Disease), Psychoneuroimmunology (Cancer, HIV/AIDS)”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Health-promoting behaviors\nA: definition and scope of Health-promoting behaviors; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Health-compromising behaviors?\nA: Health-compromising behaviors is a focused concept within “Health: Health promoting and health compromising behaviors, Life style and Chronic diseases (Diabetes, Hypertension, Coronary Heart Disease), Psychoneuroimmunology (Cancer, HIV/AIDS)”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Health-compromising behaviors\nA: definition and scope of Health-compromising behaviors; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Lifestyle?\nA: Lifestyle is a focused concept within “Health: Health promoting and health compromising behaviors, Life style and Chronic diseases (Diabetes, Hypertension, Coronary Heart Disease), Psychoneuroimmunology (Cancer, HIV/AIDS)”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Lifestyle\nA: definition and scope of Lifestyle; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Diabetes?\nA: Diabetes is a focused concept within “Health: Health promoting and health compromising behaviors, Life style and Chronic diseases (Diabetes, Hypertension, Coronary Heart Disease), Psychoneuroimmunology (Cancer, HIV/AIDS)”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Diabetes\nA: definition and scope of Diabetes; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application",
          "references": "",
          "detailed_notes": "Build a health-psychology map linking behaviours and lifestyle with chronic disease and psychoneuroimmunology.\n\nATTACHED BOOK CONNECTION\nBiology of Behavior: The biology chapter explains how the nervous and endocrine systems contribute to behavior. It covers neuron structure and communication, neural impulses, synaptic transmission and neurotransmitters, and distinguishes central and peripheral nervous-system functions. It also introduces major brain structures, the autonomic nervous system, endocrine glands and biological influences on behavior such as arousal, stress, sleep and reproduction.\nMotivation: The motivation chapter treats motives as forces behind action and explains their interaction with learning. It distinguishes biological drives from general and acquired motives, discusses unconscious motivation, and examines needs such as autonomy, achievement and related motives. The chapter also introduces Maslow's self-actualization and Frankl's emphasis on meaning, linking motivation with broader questions about purpose and human potential.\nEmotions: The emotions chapter treats emotions as experiences that include motivational, physiological and psychological dimensions. It examines basic dimensions of emotion, major theories including James-Lange and competing approaches, and the relationship between emotion, action and cognition. It also covers stress and health, including chronic stress and Type A behavior, and explains approach-approach, approach-avoidance, double approach-avoidance and avoidance-avoidance conflicts.\nAbnormal Psychology: The abnormal psychology chapter introduces criteria used to understand maladaptive or pathological behavior and discusses classification of mental disorders. It surveys anxiety, dissociative, mood, psychotic, personality and other disorders, while comparing biological, psychodynamic, learning and other explanatory viewpoints. The chapter emphasizes that abnormality cannot be reduced to a single criterion such as deviation from a norm.",
